<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">World Health Organization</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as WHO</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The World Health Organization is the specialised agency of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> for international public health, founded on 7 April 1948 with its headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>. It sets health standards, coordinates responses to epidemics and supports national health systems. Its campaigns led to the eradication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>, declared in 1980, and it coordinated the international response to the COVID-19 pandemic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/world-health-organization.jpg" alt="World Health Organization" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guilhem Vellut from Annecy, France · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:World_Health_Organization_@_Pregny-Chamb%C3%A9sy_(50636746028).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>International cooperation on disease began with the International Sanitary Conferences, the first of which met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 23 June 1851 and which continued until 1938. The first was almost solely concerned with cholera. With the causes of epidemic diseases still disputed, agreement was hard to reach: seven conferences over 41 years were convened before any produced a multi-state agreement. The seventh, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1892, finally resulted in a convention on the sanitary control of shipping through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a>, and in 1897 a convention on bubonic plague was signed by sixteen of the nineteen states attending.</p><p>The conferences led to the founding of the Pan-American Sanitary Bureau in 1902 and the Office International d'Hygiène Publique in 1907, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> created its own Health Organization in 1920. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> assembled the health organisations to form the WHO. The World Health Organization was established on 7 April 1948 and began its work on 1 September 1948, taking over the assets and duties of the League's Health Organization and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> office, including the International Classification of Diseases.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The organisation is governed by the World Health Assembly, made up of its member states, which elects an executive board of 34 health specialists, selects the director-general and approves the budget. Its funds come mainly from member states, and then from private donors. The headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> was designed by the Swiss architect Jean Tschumi and inaugurated in 1966. Its regional divisions were created between 1949 and 1952, following the model of the Pan American Health Organization, and it has six regional offices and 150 country offices.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The organisation did much to bring about the eradication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>, the near-eradication of polio and the development of an Ebola vaccine. It launched its Global Polio Eradication Initiative in 1988, working with Rotary International, the US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention and UNICEF, and helped to reduce cases by 99%. In the 1970s it dropped its commitment to a global malaria eradication campaign as too ambitious. Since the late 20th century, new actors in global health such as the World Bank and the Gates Foundation have weakened its role as coordinator, and it also coordinated the international response to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covid-19-pandemic">COVID-19 pandemic</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “World Health Organization” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its sovereignty was recognised by 95 countries as of January 1975, but its admission to the United Nations was vetoed by the Soviet Union; it held observer status at the General Assembly and belonged to the <b>World Health Organization</b>, UNESCO, the World Bank, the IMF and other agencies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-vietnam">Republic of Vietnam · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Universal Postal Union was established at Bern in 1874, the League of Nations met in Geneva from 1920, and after 1945 the city hosted the European office of the United Nations, the <b>World Health Organization</b> and many other bodies, while CERN was founded beside it in 1954.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>World Health Organization</b> described the coronavirus outbreak as a pandemic, with cases in over 100 countries.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-11">11 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
